hi this is dr. Gregory Sadler I'm a  professor of philosophy and the  president and founder of an educational  consulting company called reason IO  where we put philosophy into practice  I've studied and taught philosophy for  over 20 years and I find that many  people run into difficulties reading  classic philosophical texts sometimes  it's the way things are said or how the  text is structured but the concepts  themselves are not always that  complicated and that's where I come in  to help students and lifelong learners  I've been producing longer lecture  videos and posting them to youtube many  viewers say they find them useful what  you're currently watching is part of a  new series of shorter videos each of  them focused on one core concept from an  important philosophical text I hope you  find it useful as well one key aspect of  the stoic approach to human beings and  their relationships with each other  their situatedness within communities  and also their place in the larger scope  of the universe is an attitude that we  can call cosmopolitanism they're not the  first people to come up with that word  as a matter of fact you see some of the  cynics Diogenes for example talking  about himself being a holy chase a  citizen of the cosmos which can be  understood as the order the world or the  universe now the question that we want  to think about is what does it really  mean to be a citizen of the world what  does it mean to have the world as one's  community or as one's city is is how we  could actually translate it so there's a  lot of the aspects to this that you see  running throughout that matiee toises  discourses he doesn't have a chapter  specifically on cosmopolitanism but it's  something that he sets forth as an ideal  and I'd like to read you one short  passage that gives you sort of a good  starting point this isn't book 3 it's  chapter 4 24 he says shall we not mean  ourselves  at last and call to mind what we have  heard from the philosophers if indeed we  did not listen to them as some chanters  when they said that this university has  bought a single state but a single  organization and the substance out of  which it has been fashioned a single and  it must needs be that there is a certain  periodic change in a giving place of one  thing to another and that some things be  dissolved and others come into being  some things to remain at the same place  others to be moved that's one way to  think about it he talks about taking a  very broad point of view where we see  the whole universe not only the earth  itself the whole universe as one giant  system that we're all parts of now when  you adopt that sort of point of view it  makes the things that were engaged in as  individuals as specific people seem  rather small and so I want to clarify  from the beginning that cosmopolitanism  does not mean not attending to one  specific relationship so if one is a  child or a parent a brother or sister a  friend these things actually matter  they're not lost or swallowed up within  the great whole as a matter of fact we  make our contribution to the entire  cosmos by how we conduct ourselves in  these determinate relationships that we  have been given they have there are a  lot of many ways are dependent on what  might appear to be chance the Stoics  would actually say Providence would  think the fact that I was born in this  place the fact that I went to school  with this person and became friends with  them all those sorts of things the fact  that I lived next door to this person as  my my neighbor so it doesn't mean that  we don't attend to our individual duties  that are indicated to us by our roles  and relation  as a matter of fact what it means is  that we adopt a broader and fuller  perspective in which those can have  their their real meaning and in many  respects if we isolate ourselves against  the rest of humanity just within our  little nuclear family or a blended  family or whatever kind of family we  have or our circle of friends our clique  or our neighborhood or even our nation  we are in a certain sense not living a  full life we are not being part of that  that grand connection and communication  with all other rational beings and even  the rest of the cosmos living things you  know beautiful things that we can  experience things that we could we can  improve so it's really about adopting  this broader and fuller perspective one  aspect of this that connects directly to  these specific relationships is that we  should view and treat others equally as  parts or as children of God this is you  you might say this is a very religious  point of view Epictetus is probably one  of the most religious of the Stoics it's  not quite the same thing as the kind of  spirituality or a religiousness that you  would expect from Christians and Jews  and Muslims but there are some things in  common with it he does think that we are  all of us as individuals as human beings  at least in the higher part of ourselves  the Faculty of choice the rationality we  are parts of God we contain the divine  within ourselves so that means that when  I not only take care of the divine  within me but I say not you know eating  too much and not mixing it with with you  know indiscriminate sexuality or the  sorts of things that he talks about in  another video  by the human relationships of the divine  we also want to think about the other  people that were encountering as equally  that way so I can honor the divine in  another person by treating them in the  way that they ought to be treated by me  as a rational being that doesn't mean  that they're going to actually exercise  their rationality  he has got plenty of examples you know  he's his father and brother in this  business prime examples where perhaps  your father doesn't treat you the way a  father should but you treat your father  the way that you ought to in part  because you're treating your father is  something more than just that  mean-spirited sob that you're dealing  with right or the same thing with your  sibling your brother is trying to  deprive you of your share of the estate  or you know that's not so common these  days but your brother's trying to get  more attention than you and taking up  more of your parents time than you know  really he deserves and he thinks that  he's doing just the right thing and  doing so well you don't have control  over that but you can still treat your  brother as somebody who matters  Epictetus actually extends this as far  as how we treat those who are in  subordinate positions to ourselves in  the ancient times they had slavery or  servitude in our time we have you know  people who are subordinated to us in  different ways people who are you know  working for us perhaps or who have to  deal with us and do we treat them the  way that they ought to be treated or do  we treat them as somehow lesser and were  their feelings their thoughts don't  don't matter this is a very important  consideration so if we're doing that  then we are going to be attending to our  specific duties our specific  relationships and our roles and we're  going to place it in a larger context  it's not just about you know what's  going on between me and that person it's  not just about how you know that's  affecting me or tie  either it's about the broader community  that were part of in the better parts of  that community that they are equally a  member of whether they're using it or  not as we are perhaps they're actually  using it for kind of thing another way  that he talks about it is that for the  person who actually desires to be a part  of it if we open our eyes we are part of  a divine household a Commonwealth a a  state that contains all sorts of friends  for us humans and gods God with a small  G meaning the great you know powerful  things that help keep the universe going  I suppose but any sort of rational being  that really wants to be rational we can  be friends with epictetus thinks that  that wars and stripes arise between us  in part because we prioritize our own  interest which is a legitimate thing our  own self-interest we prioritize that  over everything else and we think that  that's where our genuine good lies all  the time to the exclusion of others and  so things are allowed to come between us  I've done video about that as well that  you can take a look at so you know when  nations go to war with each other it's  because they're not being cosmopolitan  they're not treating each other as if  they they are all genuinely part of one  great household one great the economy  weakling or economic a in the Greek  another thing that he says is that one  can live well anywhere and I've got a  few passages that I want to bring up  from from that in boat to chapter 16 he  has a personal lamenting when then shall  I see Athens once more and the Acropolis  and he says poor man are you're not  satisfied with what you see  every day have you anything finer or  greater to look at than the sun the moon  the stars the whole earth the sea and if  you really understand him who governs  the universe and bear him about within  you do you not yearn for bits do not yet  yearn for bits of stone one had a pretty  rock meaning the Acropolis when  therefore you are about to leave the  sudden moon what will you do will you  sit and cry as little children cry why  are you calling yourself a philosopher  if that's the case we can live well  pretty much anywhere  there will be more challenges of course  in many places but if we can pour  ourselves as rational beings we can get  along with anybody else who wants to be  rational and the universe itself is full  of wonderful things that we can  collaborate with them and cooperate with  a little bit later he says this is a  book 3 chapter 24 again all things are  full of friends first gods and then also  men who by nature have been made one  household with another and that some men  must remain with each other others must  depart and then though we must rejoice  and those who dwell with us yet we must  not grieve at those who depart we not  only have to be willing to accept in our  own capacity to live well anywhere but  we also need to understand that other  people require them as well that perhaps  they need to go somewhere and we  shouldn't center it entirely on  ourselves and how we feel and how we  reflected we should understand ourselves  as part of a larger system again just to  stress this is the last point kind of  Napolitan ISM doesn't mean that we don't  focus on the particular that we don't  care about any of the parts but it means  adopting this broader view in which we  are able to see things the way that at  least to a certain extent God might see  them where each thing  and each thing has its place and each  thing is connected up with everything  else
 

